  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single nature reserve in Beijing, which may limit generalizability to protected areas with different trail infrastructures or visitor demographics.",
      "what": "This study analyzed over 18 million GPS trajectory points aggregated into a 30-meter spatial grid to map hiking dynamics in the Beijing Baihua Mountain National Nature Reserve across annual, daily, and hourly scales. The analysis revealed that hiking is highly concentrated in just 11.06% of the reserve, with topography and vegetation driving the spatial patterns. Temporally, hiking is highly intermittent, with no activity recorded in 57.62% of hourly intervals, while peak hourly intensity in a single pixel exceeded the reserve's annual mean intensity by over seven orders of magnitude.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that recreational pressure on protected mountain ecosystems is highly concentrated in both space and time rather than being evenly distributed. This high-resolution quantification allows conservationists to pinpoint specific times and micro-locations where human-wildlife conflicts are most likely to occur."
    }
  },
